\f1\fs22 As you step through the weathered, once-gilded double doors, you enter a grand foyer that echoes with the whispers of its opulent past. The once vibrant wallpaper, displaying a lush pattern of roses intertwined with golden vines, is now peeling at the corners, revealing the cracked plaster beneath. Dimly lit by tarnished chandeliers, their crystals coated in decades of dust, the room is bathed in a melancholy, amber light.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the center of the room, a large circular spa bath demands attention, its once gleaming, marble edges now stained with age and neglect. Intricate mosaics of mermaids and sea creatures, their colors faded, grace the bottom of the empty pool. The gold-plated fixtures are tarnished, their brilliance dimmed by time and neglect.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the right, a bar stretches the length of the room, its mahogany counter worn smooth by countless patrons. Behind it, a massive mirror, its silver backing spotted and fading, reflects the room in a ghostly tableau. Crystal decanters, their contents long since evaporated, sit on the dusty shelves, a testament to hedonistic nights past.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Plush, red velvet curtains hang heavily against the far wall, their fabric moth-eaten and faded. Behind them, a grand staircase, its balustrade ornately carved but chipped and splintered, spirals up to the second floor, where an array of doors, each once marked by a lady's name, line a long, dimly lit hallway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The air is thick with the scent of stale perfume, a lingering reminder of the women who once laughed and lived within these walls. The once vibrant space, resounding with laughter, music, and whispered promises, is now eerily silent, filled only with the echoes of its own grandeur. Despite its decline, the brothel retains an air of decadent charm, a poignant testament to its glamorous, albeit tarnished, past.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pushing open those creaky, ornate doors, the kind you'd imagine guarding the entryway to a retired burlesque queen's boudoir, you'd find yourself standing in a room where Time had thrown a raucous party, then left without cleaning up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The wallpaper, once as vibrant as a rose garden at high noon, now sagged in the corners, its design of intertwining gold and flowers looking like forgotten love letters to a bygone era. Shy light, as if embarrassed by the room's decline, puddled down from chandeliers that hung like cobwebbed stalactites, their once brilliant crystals dulled by years of disregard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room's belle of the ball, a grandiose spa bath, lay in the center. It was a circular dance floor where water had once waltzed with naked skin. The faded mosaics decorating its bottom were like the bleached tattoos of an ancient sailor, depicting mermaids seeming more world-weary than whimsical. The fixtures, once gleaming gold, had traded their luster for the hue of an old pirate doubloon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Off to the side, a once proud bar now slouched along the wall, its mahogany top worn smooth from years of supporting elbow-bent confessions. An expansive mirror behind it, spotted and worn, reflected the room like an old vaudeville performer squinting through cataracts. Ghostly decanters stood guard, their spirits evaporated, leaving only the memory of a more intoxicating time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heavy curtains, the color of a cabaret singer's lipstick, hung faded and moth-eaten against the wall, leading the eye to a grand staircase. The staircase, its balustrade as intricately carved as a Victorian cameo but bruised by time, spiraled upwards to a corridor filled with doors that seemed to sigh under the weight of their own stories.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The air was thick with the scent of yesteryear's perfume, like a stubborn rumor that refused to be silenced. The decadent silence was broken only by the ghostly echoes of laughter, whispers and promises that had long since evaporated into the ether. And yet, despite the heartache of its decline, there was a rakish charm about the place, a wistful wink from a forgotten courtesan, forever captured in the folds of faded velvet.
